## Azure Well-Architected pillars (enterprise)

Apply in HLD/LLTD and review IaC against these lenses:

| Pillar | GDH minimum bar |
| --- | --- |
| **Reliability** | Defined RTO/RPO; zone-redundant or documented single-zone risk; health probes; retry/idempotent deploy |
| **Security** | MCSB + BIO 2.0 baseline; identity-first; private connectivity; see `security.mdc` |
| **Cost Optimization** | Right-size SKUs; tags for chargeback; dev/test auto-shutdown where applicable |
| **Operational Excellence** | IaC + ADO pipelines; observability to central LAW; runbooks for platform patterns |
| **Performance Efficiency** | SKU choice justified in LLTD; autoscale where variable load |

## Landing zone & connectivity (CAF)

- **Hub-spoke** for Profile A/B2; Profile A3/PaaS-only documents trust boundaries instead of full §3.4 inventory.
- **Central egress** via Azure Firewall; UDRs to hub; no spoke bypass unless ADR.
- **Private DNS** zones linked to hub/spoke VNets; avoid public endpoints for PaaS when PE available.
- **Subscription vending** via approved product lines; one application landing zone per workload boundary.
- **Platform vs application** subscriptions separated; platform team owns hub, firewall, DNS, central LAW.
- **Management groups:** GDH uses max non-breaking Deny + DINE/Modify; Legacy is Audit-only during migration. Document in LLTD.

## Resilience & DR (document in LLTD §7)

- **Backup:** Azure Backup or native service backup for stateful tiers; retention meets BIV/legal hold.
- **Region:** single-region default unless HLD mandates paired region; document failover gap if single-region.
- **Immutable logs** for security/audit streams where BIO requires tamper evidence.
- **Dependencies:** explicit upstream/downstream (Entra, DNS, firewall, Key Vault) in context diagram.

## Platform automation patterns

- **Deployment patterns:** orchestration + configuration repos; no fork for one consumer.
- **Policy-as-code:** MCSB/BIO via Bicep in orchestration and configuration governance folders; exemptions time-boxed in Git. No EPAC.
- **Identity per pattern:** dedicated pipeline MI/SP per stage; WIF only.
- **Observability:** diagnostic settings deployed with resource; LAW workspace per landing zone or central platform LAW.

## Subscription & management group design (CAF)

- **Platform subscriptions:** connectivity (hub), identity (if dedicated), management (LAW, automation, ACR), security (Sentinel, Defender export).
- **Application landing zones:** one workload boundary per application subscription unless HLD consolidates tightly coupled tiers.
- **Management group hierarchy:** root → platform → landing zones → sandbox; GDH MG for MCSB/BIO with max non-breaking Deny + remediation; Legacy MG Audit-only during migration.
- **Policy inheritance:** assign MCSB + NL BIO Cloud Theme V2 at GDH MG; avoid subscription-level duplication unless exception documented.
- **RBAC at MG scope:** platform team Reader/Contributor on app subs only when operational need exists; prefer RG-scoped roles.

Reference: [CAF resource organization](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/cloud-adoption-framework/ready/landing-zone/design-area/resource-org) · [Azure landing zone architecture](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/guide/landing-zones/)

## Network architecture patterns

| Pattern | Use when | Document in LLTD |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Hub-spoke** | Profile A/B2 with VNet integration | §3.4 full inventory, UDRs, NSG rules |
| **PaaS-only (A3)** | No custom VNet; public endpoints avoided via PE where available | §3.3a trust boundaries, traffic summary table |
| **Private Link** | PaaS data plane (Storage, SQL, Key Vault, Web Apps) | PE subnet, DNS zone links, firewall rules |
| **Central egress** | All spoke outbound via Azure Firewall | FQDN tags, IDPS, logging to LAW |
| **Ingress WAF** | Internet-facing HTTP(S) | App Gateway/Front Door + WAF mode (Prevention in prod) |

**DNS:** private DNS zones in hub; link to spokes; avoid split-horizon mistakes (document forwarders in §3.4).

**IP addressing:** RFC1918 space planned centrally; no overlapping CIDRs between spokes; reserve PE subnet per spoke.

## Identity architecture (Entra + Azure RBAC)

- **Human access:** Entra groups → Azure RBAC roles; no direct user assignments at scale.
- **Workload access:** user-assigned MI per pipeline stage; federated credentials for ADO (no secrets).
- **Cross-tenant:** canary pipeline identities live in canary tenant only; no shared SP across tenants.
- **Guest/B2B:** document in LLTD if partners access app data; Conditional Access for guests.

## Observability architecture

- **Central Log Analytics workspace** (platform) receives diagnostics from hub, firewalls, Key Vault audit, Entra sign-in (where exported).
- **Application insights** per workload or shared per landing zone per LLTD §5 decision.
- **Metrics:** platform alerts on firewall drop spikes, Key Vault access anomalies, failed deployments.
- **Retention:** align with BIO 8.15 (operational ≥180 days; security/legal hold longer when required).
- **Immutability:** use immutability policies on storage holding audit archives when tamper evidence required.

## Data classification & residency

- **Allowed regions:** policy-enforced (EU/NL where required); document in §0 metadata.
- **Data stores:** map BIV level to encryption, PE requirement, backup retention, and access logging intensity.
- **Citizen / special-category data (A2):** DPIA reference, DigiD/eHerkenning broker, minimal retention, Hoog BIV triggers CISO review.

## AI / Azure OpenAI workloads (when in scope)

- Follow **MCSB AI domain** (AI-1..AI-7): approved models, content safety, agent least privilege, monitoring, red teaming for Hoog BIV.
- Prefer **Azure AI landing zone** pattern: dedicated subscription or RG boundary, private networking, managed identity to model endpoints.
- Document prompt/logging boundaries: no citizen PII in model logs unless DPIA allows.

Reference: [Azure OpenAI landing zone](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/openai/architecture/openai-landing-zone)

## Integration & G2G (Forum Standaardisatie)

- **Digikoppeling / DigiD / eHerkenning:** use approved municipal broker/integration layer; do not custom-build citizen auth.
- **API management:** APIM as external façade; OAuth/JWT validation; rate limiting; mTLS for high-trust partners when required.
- Document external endpoints, data flows, and certificate rotation in LLTD §3.3 and §4.

## Capacity & lifecycle

- **SKU justification:** record in LLTD §3 or §8 NFR table; right-size for cost pillar.
- **Autoscale:** define min/max and scale rules for variable workloads; load test before production.
- **Lifecycle:** decommission procedure (backup export, RBAC removal, DNS cleanup, policy exemption removal) in §7 or runbook.
- **Environment promotion:** dev → tst → acc → prd with increasing policy strictness; no prod secrets in lower envs.

## Architecture review red flags (escalate to Design Authority)

- New internet-exposed PaaS without WAF or ADR
- Shared keys or connection strings in IaC for production
- Single-region stateful tier without documented RPO gap
- Policy exemption without expiry or CISO path for Deny controls
- Fork of orchestration repo for one consumer instead of configuration parameter file
- AI workload processing special-category data without DPIA and MCSB AI controls